 is the primary key, and there are no other indexes.\n\n**Scans the query forces**\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`.** `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The database has to evaluate `lower()` on every row.\n2. **Full scan of `orders`, or a hash join over all of it.** Nothing guarantees an index on `orders.user_id`, and foreign keys don't create one in Postgres or MySQL/InnoDB (InnoDB does add one automatically). Without it, the join reads the whole table to find one user's orders.\n3. **Wide row fetches from `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables, so an index-only scan is impossible. It also returns duplicate column names (`id`, `created_at` and so on), which can silently overwrite each other in many client libraries.\n\n**Fixes**\n\nIndex the lookup. Pick one of these:\n\n```sql\n-- Option A: expression index (Postgres; MySQL 8+ supports functional indexes too)\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- Option B: case-insensitive type, with no function needed in queries (Postgres)\nALTER TABLE users ALTER COLUMN email TYPE citext;\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_idx ON users (email);\n```\n\nThen index the join key:\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\nThen rewrite the query to name its columns:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\nThe `WHERE` clause must match the expression index exactly, so keep `lower(u.email)`. With option B, write `u.email = 'a@example.org'` instead.\n\nWith both indexes, the plan becomes one index lookup on `users` and one index range scan on `orders`. Both are O(log n), and neither table is scanned in full.\n\n**Optional**\n\n- If you only list orders for the user, a composite index such as `(user_id, created_at DESC)` also covers ordering.\n- If emails are normalized to lowercase at write time, you can drop `lower()` and use a plain unique index. A `CHECK (email = lower(email))` constraint would enforce that.\n\nI didn't edit `query.sql`. Do you want me to apply the rewrite? If you share the schema, I can confirm the column names and check which indexes already exist.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}